# Field Guide: Preparing a Home for a 72-Hour Power Outage

Prepare an ordinary household to get safely and comfortably through an outage of up to 72 hours. Assumes a temperate climate and no backup generator. Work the sections in order; earlier ones matter most.

## Water
- 4 litres per person per day = 12 litres per person for 72 hours; half for drinking, half for cooking and basic hygiene.
- Food-grade containers, kept out of direct sunlight; replace stored water every 6 months.
- If a storm is forecast, also fill the bathtub — bathtub water is for flushing toilets and washing, never drinking.

## Food
- A dedicated shelf of non-perishable food for 3 days: canned beans, canned fish, peanut butter, crackers, dried fruit, shelf-stable milk.
- Pick foods needing no cooking; own a manual can opener, because an electric one is useless.
- Eat in order: perishable fridge food first, then the freezer, then the emergency shelf.

## Refrigerator and freezer
- Unopened fridge stays safe about 4 hours; a full freezer about 48 hours, but a half-full freezer only about 24 hours — so keep the freezer consolidated and full, even with containers of frozen water.
- Keep both doors closed as much as possible; tape a reminder note on each door.
- Discard any perishable food over 2 hours above 4 degrees Celsius; when in doubt, throw it out.

## Light
- Battery-powered LED lanterns or headlamps as primary light; avoid candles entirely, since they are a leading cause of house fires during outages.
- At least one light per person, plus two spare sets of batteries for each light.
- One headlamp beside each bed and one lantern in the kitchen, placed to be found in the dark.

## Heat and safety
- Never run a camping stove, barbecue, or generator indoors, in a garage, or near a window: each produces carbon monoxide, which is odourless and can be fatal.
- Install at least one battery-powered carbon monoxide alarm on each floor where people sleep.
- In cold weather, gather everyone in one small south-facing room with its door closed; layered clothing and blankets are safer than any improvised heater.
- If the indoor temperature falls below 10 degrees Celsius and you cannot stay warm, move to a warming centre or a neighbour's home.

## Communication and information
- Battery-powered or hand-crank radio for emergency broadcasts; know your local emergency station's frequency beforehand.
- Whenever a storm is forecast, charge every phone and power bank — a full 20,000 mAh power bank recharges a typical phone about 4 times.
- Put your phone in low-power mode at the start of the outage.
- Agree in advance on one out-of-area contact whom every household member texts, because texts get through congested networks more reliably than voice calls.

## Medical needs
- A first-aid kit and a 7-day supply of every prescription medication; rotate them so they stay in date.
- Refrigerated medication such as insulin generally stays usable at room temperature for up to 28 days, but confirm the rule for your specific medication with a pharmacist beforehand.
- Write your pharmacy, doctor, and poison-control numbers on paper, because a dead phone is not a phone book.

## When the power returns
- Wait 5 minutes before switching major appliances back on, so the grid can stabilise.
- Check the freezer: food that still contains ice crystals can be safely refrozen.
- Restock everything used within one week, starting with water and batteries.
- Write a one-page note on what was missing or awkward, and fix at least one gap before the next outage.
